cartridge "HEAT SEEKER/J.WOLVERTON/1985"

' HEAT SEEKER
' A ColecoVision game based on "Heat Seeker" by Jeff Wolverton, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette, March 1985 (Commodore 64 machine language, SYS 2061; the same
' issue had a VIC-20 BASIC version). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI
' made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: you fly a jet over an enemy base of ten heat-seeking
' missiles standing on their pads. Strafe them on the ground; but every
' little while the last missile on the right lifts off and chases the heat
' of your engine. It flies faster than you, you turn tighter. You cannot
' shoot it down: dodge it, or lead it into a missile still on its pad.
' When every pad is empty the base is cleared: 1000 points and the ship
' is given back. One or two players (taking turns), eight ships each.
'
' From the original (read from its machine code, orig/hseek64.dis):
' - one physics step moves every object by its velocity vector (length 85,
'   in 1/256 pixel units) times a speed factor: the jet 4/5/6 (novice,
'   intermediate, expert), the missile 6/7/8, the shots 11 (see
'   Liberties for the speeds used here);
' - the stick turns the jet's vector by K/256 radian a step, K = 10/11/13;
'   the missile turns toward the jet by 9/10/11 a step (the side picked
'   from the cross product of its heading and the jet's direction);
' - the jet starts heading right; a new missile lifts off 128 steps after
'   the round starts or the last one blew up, from the right-most full pad
'   (the pad is emptied), heading straight up;
' - fire: one shot every 28 steps while the button is held, at most six
'   on screen, each lives 127 steps; a shot that reaches a full pad
'   destroys it (50 points) and burns there for the rest of its life;
' - the missile kills the jet when both centres are within 16 C64 pixels
'   (here 10, the sprites being smaller); the jet dies touching a full pad;
'   the missile that touches a full pad blows up (100 points, the pad
'   stays);
' - 10 points every 256 steps survived; all pads empty and no missile
'   left: 1000 points and the ship back; the pads are filled again at each
'   new round (after a crash too); two players alternate every round;
' - the screen wraps left-right, the top and bottom stop the flyers.
'
' Liberties: one physics step per video frame, at 3/4 of a C64 step
' (speeds and turn rates x 3/4, the step counts 128/28/127/256 x 4/3 =
' 171/37/169/341), so the turning radii are the C64's; the C64 sets its
' CIA timer to about 280 interrupts a second, which would move everything
' 4.7 times as fast as a frame-per-step port: unplayable on a TV. On
' NOVICE and INTERMEDIATE the missile's speed factor is 5 and 6 (C64: 6
' and 7): a bot reacting 12 frames late could not escape the C64's ratio;
' EXPERT keeps the C64's 8 against the jet's 6. Also eased on NOVICE /
' INTERMEDIATE only (playability pass, a 12-frame-lag bot): the launch
' countdown runs at 1/2 / 2/3 speed (342 / 256 steps), the missile turns
' 7 / 8 (C64 9 / 10) and runs out of fuel after 4 / 6 seconds (240 / 360
' steps), blowing up harmlessly for 10 points (the C64's missile flew until
' it hit something). EXPERT is the C64's (at 3/4 time); the field is the
' ColecoVision's 208 x 160 pixels inside the safe area (the C64's 320 x
' 200 overscan field), the wrap jumps between columns 2 and 29 so no
' sprite ever crosses the TV's cropped edges; the ten pads stand in five
' pairs (the C64's 40 columns spaced them evenly); the jet's heading is
' kept as an angle (the C64 rotated the vector, the same motion);
' collisions are box tests (the C64 used its sprite/background collision
' register); left and right on the stick turn the jet too (the C64 used
' only up = clockwise, down = anticlockwise, which still work); a missile
' in flight sharpens its turn by 1 every 256 steps (at most to the jet's
' rate), a guard so a jet circling tightly cannot farm the survival
' points for ever (bot.mjs MODE=circle dies within 12 s); a missile that touches the top or the floor
' slides along it as on the C64; a short wait and READY message between
' rounds (the C64 waited for a button press too); sound effects (the C64
' had its shot zap, a hum and explosions); GAME OVER then the title (the
' C64 asked PLAY AGAIN? Y/N).
'
' Controls: controller 1 or 2 (both work for both players, as on the
' C64): left/right (or up/down) turn the jet, a side button fires (hold it
' for a stream of shots). On the title: up/down choose a line, left/right
' change the skill or the number of players, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128;
' frame loop protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off and the
' display off; music first in the sound table, played once.

